1a. Benchmark reference invocations
-----------------------------------

The performance comparison harness in `docs/bench/` executes reference
tools as subprocesses. It never links reference implementations into the
crate and never redistributes their sources or binaries.

- Distribution-provided tools (gzip, zstd, xz, bzip2, lzip, lz4, lzop,
  lrzip, rzip, brotli, ncompress, zpaq, 7-Zip, tar, cpio, ar, zip,
  bsdtar, cabextract, unsquashfs, unar, lhasa, and the Python standard library)
  are invoked only for measurement. Their package version and, where
  available, compiler comment are recorded in the generated report.
- Pinned source builds under `docs/bench/refs/build.py`:
  - ZX0/ZX1/ZX2/ZX5 (Einar Saukas, BSD-3-Clause)
  - lzsa (Emmanuel Marty et al., zlib license; src/matchfinder.c is CC0)
  - liblzf (Marc Alexander Lehmann, BSD-2-Clause OR GPL-2.0-or-later)
  - FastLZ (Ariya Hidayat, MIT)
  - Snappy 1.2.1 (Google, BSD-3-Clause)
  These sources are fetched into `target/bench/refs/src/` and are not
  committed. The exact CFLAGS, compiler, source revision, and binary
  sha256 are written to `target/bench/refs/build-info.json`.
- Existing "test-time oracle" statements in this file include the
  benchmark use when the same tool is listed. GPL/LGPL reference tools
  remain execution-only; no conversion path or code incorporation is
  created by benchmarking.

1. Specification-driven implementations
---------------------------------------

The following implementations are intended to follow published format
specifications or standards documents:

- DEFLATE: RFC 1951. The encoder's bit-length repair
  (`src/compress/deflate/encode.rs`) follows the bit-length-count
  scheme of zlib's `gen_bitlen` (zlib license), reimplemented in
  Rust with no code copied. The decoder's over-subscribed
  Huffman-tree rejection (`HuffmanTable::from_lengths`) uses a
  Kraft-sum left-tracking counter arithmetically equivalent to zlib
  `inflate_table`'s `left` accounting; the check is the standard
  Kraft-inequality validation per RFC 1951 §3.2.2. HDIST values of
  31/32 are accepted when the reserved distance-code lengths are
  zero, per the RFC 1951 §3.2.7 strict reading rather than zlib's
  `hdist > 30` rejection. The Python `zlib` module (CPython, PSF
  License) is also invoked as a local test-time interoperability
  oracle for raw deflate streams (`tests/integration/deflate.rs`,
  wbits=-15); it is not linked or distributed
- gzip: RFC 1952. The system `gzip` binary (GNU gzip,
  GPL-3.0-or-later) is invoked only as a local test-time
  interoperability oracle (integration tests and the
  archive-fuzz pipe oracle) and fixture generator
  (`tests/fixtures/gzip/`); it is not linked or distributed
- zlib: RFC 1950. The Python `zlib` module (CPython, PSF License)
  is invoked only as a local test-time interoperability oracle;
  it is not linked or distributed
- zstd: RFC 8878. The upstream reference implementation is dual
  BSD-3-Clause OR GPLv2 licensed; Meta's earlier BSD+PATENTS grant was
  removed by that 2017 relicensing. The system `zstd` CLI is used only
  as a local test-time oracle (integration tests) and fixture generator
  for `tests/fixtures/zstd/zstd_*.zst`; it is not linked or distributed
- Brotli stream format: RFC 7932
  - The encoder and decoder are spec-informed implementations written
    against RFC 7932; no code was copied from the reference Brotli
    implementation. Encoder features include LZ77 command generation,
    literal context modeling, multi-block-type metablocks, simple and
    complex prefix codes, and static dictionary references
  - The system `brotli` binary (google/brotli, MIT) is run locally as a
    test-time interoperability oracle and fixture generator
    (`tests/fixtures/brotli/`); it is not linked or distributed
  - Patent/IPR note: Google has disclosed an IPR declaration for
    RFC 7932 (https://datatracker.ietf.org/ipr/2396/); this
    implementation follows the RFC directly and introduces no new
    algorithmic extension. Google's second declaration (IETF
    Datatracker #3147, US2017036372 "context-dependent shared
    dictionaries") covers only the shared-dictionary extension
    (draft-vandevenne-shared-brotli-format), which is out of scope
    for this implementation
- LZSA: format documentation (StreamFormat.md, BlockFormat_LZSA1.md,
  BlockFormat_LZSA2.md) and reference implementation by Emmanuel Marty
  et al. (zlib license; src/matchfinder.c is CC0). Where the written
  specification is ambiguous (extended-length marker values, the LZSA2
