2. Reference implementations and de facto format definitions
-------------------------------------------------------------

Some formats do not have a single RFC-grade specification and are implemented
against de facto reference behavior:

- Deflate64 (ZIP method 9 / 7z method ID 0x04 0x01 0x09)
  - Reference: PKWARE APPNOTE.TXT registers the method ID only; the
    bit-level structure (64 KiB window, distance codes 30/31, length
    code 285) is defined by the de facto description embodied in
    Info-ZIP `unzip`/`inflate.c` ("Info-ZIP APPNOTE additions") and
    in the public-domain hwzip reference (see section 2)
  - Repository scope: Rust reimplementation of the Deflate64 bit
    structure; decode and encode, no upstream code copied. The encoder
    (`src/compress/deflate64/encode.rs`) reuses the crate's own DEFLATE
    encoder restricted to the DEFLATE-compatible subset (match lengths
    <= 257, 32 KiB window), which emits valid Deflate64 streams without
    exercising the extended distance/length codes
  - Patent note: Deflate64 is a circa-2001 PKWARE extension. No specific
    patent covering it has been identified in this audit, and no formal
    patent search was performed.
  - Trademark note: "Deflate64" is an unregistered PKWARE trademark;
    its use here (module and method naming) is nominative only

- ZIP legacy methods 1-6 (Shrinking, Reducing, Imploding)
  (`src/archive/zip/{unshrink,expand,implode,lzmatch}.rs`)
  - Reference: hwzip 2.4 by Hans Wennborg
    (https://www.hanshq.net/zip.html; code dedicated to the public
    domain under the Unlicense), vendored read-only at
    `.local/refs/hwzip/` (not part of the repository). The companion
    article "Shrink, Reduce, and Implode" is (c) Hans Wennborg with no
    explicit grant - consulted as documentation only. Info-ZIP
    `unshrink.c`/`unreduce.c`/`explode.c` were also consulted as decode
    references.
  - Provenance: ported - `unshrink.rs` (shrink.c), `expand.rs`
    (reduce.c), and `implode.rs` (implode.c) are algorithmic
    transliterations of the public-domain reference (constants, table
    layouts, the free-code queue / partial-clear semantics, and the
    follower-set cost model are carried over). `implode.rs`'s Huffman
    code-length computation and decode-table mechanics are independent
    Rust code (hwzip's huffman.c min-heap algorithm was not carried
    over). `lzmatch.rs` adapts lz77.c's hash-chain design (same Knuth
    multiplier and 2^15 table size) but implements a simpler greedy
    parse rather than hwzip's lazy matching. Compatible with AGPL-3.0
    because hwzip is dedicated to the public domain.
  - Repository scope: decode and encode for ZIP methods 1-6
    (`ZipCompression::{Shrunk, Reduce, Implode}`); the method dispatch
    in decode.rs/encode.rs is crate-original wiring.
  - Test fixtures: `tests/fixtures/zip/hw_{shrink,reduce,implode}.zip`
    were generated by running hwzip 2.4 over the crate-provided
    `payload.bin`; they are checked in unmodified as known-answer
    decode inputs and are not linked. The crate's encoder output was
    additionally verified to expand correctly under hwunshrink/
    hwexpand/hwexplode (local oracle, not distributed). `unar`
    (XADMaster) and Info-ZIP `unzip` serve as additional local decode
    oracles for Shrink/Implode/Deflate64; the Info-ZIP `zip`
    binary (same package, same license) is additionally invoked
    as a local encode-side oracle.

- FastLZ
  - Reference: `fastlz.c` reference implementation by Ariya Hidayat
    (MIT license); there is no independent written specification, so
    the reference implementation's behavior is the de facto format
    definition
  - Repository scope: Rust reimplementation informed by that observed
    behavior; no code was copied
  - Known-answer decode vectors in tests/integration/fastlz.rs were
    produced by running the reference encoder locally

- LZRW1-A
  - Reference: Ross Williams' public-domain `lzrw1-a.c`; the format is
    defined by that file's format description
  - Repository scope: Rust reimplementation of the documented format;
    the encoder's hash shares the reference's 3-byte
    mixing expression and 40543 multiplier, differing only in the
    extraction shift position
  - Known-answer decode vectors in tests/integration/lzrw1a.rs were
    produced by running the reference encoder locally after a
    small 64-bit portability patch; the patched source is not
    included in this repository

- LZRW1 / LZRW2 / LZRW3 / LZRW3-A
  - Reference: Ross Williams' public-domain `lzrw1.c`, `lzrw2.c`,
    `lzrw3.c`, `lzrw3-a.c` (and the `lzrw2.txt`, `lzrw3.txt`,
